menu "Keepsake power management"

    config KEEPSAKE_PM_DFS
        bool "Scale the CPU frequency down when idle"
        depends on PM_ENABLE
        default y
        help
            Runs the CPU at KEEPSAKE_PM_MIN_CPU_FREQ_MHZ while nothing needs speed, and at
            the full default CPU frequency while a cpu_boost::ScopedBoost is held (screen
            rendering, input handling, recording). ESP-IDF's frequency scaling does not
            follow load on its own: without a held boost the CPU stays at the minimum, so
            any latency-sensitive work must hold one. Drivers (SPI, I2S, SDMMC, Wi-Fi) keep
            the APB clock up during their transfers by themselves.

    config KEEPSAKE_PM_MIN_CPU_FREQ_MHZ
        int "Idle CPU frequency (MHz)"
        depends on KEEPSAKE_PM_DFS
        range 80 240
        default 80
        help
            80 keeps the APB clock at 80 MHz, which every peripheral here expects. 40 (XTAL)
            is not offered: it would slow the APB clock too.

    config KEEPSAKE_DEV_NO_AUTO_SLEEP
        bool "Dev build: never use automatic light sleep"
        default n
        help
            Automatic light sleep (#55) drops the USB-Serial/JTAG console whenever the chip
            sleeps. Turn this on for development builds to keep the console usable once
            automatic light sleep exists. It has no effect today: automatic light sleep is
            not enabled yet, only frequency scaling.

endmenu
